 6. 🛰️ Musk-proofing modern warfare 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Two men set up a Starlink terminal in the Donetsk region of Ukraine. Photo: Dmytro Smolienko/Ukrinform/Future Publishing via Getty Images Axios' Thomas Novelly and Colin Demarest, authors of our twice-weekly Future of Defense newsletter, write: 

 Rival satellite companies are jockeying to loosen Elon Musk's grip on battlefield communications after his company's restrictions on Ukraine's Starlink service shook officials in Europe and the U.S.

 Why it matters: The episode, which temporarily disrupted Ukraine's drone usage, shows how vulnerable a military can be to the whims of a single company or market dominator. 🌌 The big picture: Musk's SpaceX, which is the parent company of Starlink, is by far the world's leader in space-based connectivity with more than 11,000 satellites .

 Eutelsat, a French firm, is behind them with more than 600 satellites. SpaceX's massive lead has made it difficult for European countries that want to establish independence from the Musk-led company. 🔭 Zoom in: Now, non-U.S. firms seeking to compete with SpaceX are pitching their services with more urgency.

 Last week, Finnish firms ICEYE and Nokia announced an agreement with Ukraine's defense ministry to explore "sovereign satellite communications." Share this story . 

 
 
